<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel von Brukenthal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1721">1721</a>–<a href="/years/1803">1803</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel von Brukenthal was a Transylvanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> nobleman and statesman who governed the Grand Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> and Joseph II from the 1770s to 1787. A collector of paintings, books and minerals, he built a baroque palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibiu">Hermannstadt</a> (Sibiu) whose collections opened to the public in 1817 as the Brukenthal Museum, one of the oldest museums in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-von-brukenthal.jpg" alt="Samuel von Brukenthal" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samuel_von_Brukenthal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
